+# Schedule posts with a publish time checked on read
+
+## Context
+
+A post went live the moment it was saved, so authors kept finished drafts
+elsewhere and pasted them in at launch time. Editorial wants to write now
+and publish later; ops wants to read the schedule from a terminal.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `posts.published_at` is a nullable text column holding a UTC ISO 8601
+  timestamp in `toISOString()` form, like `created_at`. `NULL` or a past
+  instant means public, a future instant means scheduled.
+- **Nothing runs at publish time.** Every read compares the column with
+  the current time, so a post becomes public by the clock alone. There is
+  no job, no status flag and nothing to fall out of sync.
+- The comparison is on text, in SQL and in `isScheduled()` alike. That is
+  correct only because every value has the same form, so
+  `PostController` rewrites the request's `publishAt` (any offset) to that
+  form before saving. Rows written by hand must use it too.
+- One rule, two shapes: `Post.visibleTo(viewerId)` filters lists (posts
+  list, search, home page), `PostPolicy` guards one post. A scheduled post
+  answers **404** to everyone but its author, on `view`, `update` and
+  `delete` alike, because a 403 would confirm it exists.
+- The home page uses guest visibility for every viewer; authors find their
+  scheduled posts in the posts list.
+- `publishAt` must carry a zone (`Z` or an offset). A bare local time
+  would be read in the server's zone rather than the author's, so the
+  form converts the browser's local time to UTC before sending.
+- On `PUT /posts/:id` an absent `publishAt` clears the date, the same as
+  `null`: the request replaces the post.
+- `bun run console posts:scheduled` prints `<id>\t<title>\t<published_at>`
+  per scheduled post, soonest first, and nothing else on stdout.
+  `bin/console.ts` sends what booting logs to stderr to keep that true.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Any new query that lists posts must start from `Post.visibleTo()`;
+  `Post.newQuery()` returns scheduled posts too.
+- A cache in front of post pages or lists would need an expiry no later
+  than the next publish time, since no event fires when a post goes live.
+- Publish times depend on the server clock.
+- Tabs and line breaks in a title are printed as a space by the report.
